  <result aggregation-status="1" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual Output Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator ascending="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output Indicator</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">- Continuous engagement between the FGS and the FMS leaders 
- # of FMS submitted their submissions on the constitutional amendments 
- # of women engaged in the discussions 
- # of risks analyses prepared  
- A strategy for the phased adoption of the Constitution adopted
- # of articles redrafted based on the received submissions</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline year="2022">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">- An action plan on the implementation of the state building priorities have not been agreed yet.
- FMS institutions have not submitted their official submissions on the reviewed Provisional Constitution yet
- Political situation in Somalia constantly evolves
- Once selected, it is expected that the MPs of the new 11th parliament will not have sufficient capacity to facilitate the constitutional review process
- Following the completion of the selection process, composition of OC, MOCA and ICRIC may be changed.</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">- At least one NCC meeting organized
-At least three FMS submitted their submissions. At least 1,000 women engaged in the state building processes.
- A joint strategy for the Constitution Review Process adopted.
- 30% quota is implemented</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">- 1 NCC meeting and other high- level discussions on state building priorities organized 
- NCC in October 2022 agreed to accelerate political negotiation on the constitutional review process, focusing on issues related to federalism and nominate the members of the constitutional review bodies.
- NCC in June 2022 reafirmed that the constitutional review process is one of the key national priorities. NCC in October 2022 agreed to prioritize negotiation to complete the constitutional review process and nominate representatives in the constitutional review bodies. 
- During the first nine months of 2022 has been to complete the indirect elections and ensure peaceful transition of power. Hence, the focus of the UN has been to support consolidation of the political processes and institutional framework. Therefore, CSOs have been limitedly engaged in the constitutional review process. Starting from 2023, UNDP and UNSOM will particularly focus to expanding spectrum of partners in the constitutional reform by engaging women, youth, community, clan and religious leaders, persons with disabilities and others in the process, securing that their needs and ideas are integrated in the constitutional text.</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
